# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log

# Maintainer working directories: a notes vault and a multi-agent workspace. Neither is
# part of this project — they sit beside it and are excluded wholesale so working notes,
# session logs and operational detail cannot reach the repo. Nothing in the codebase
# depends on either; see the README for what they are.
.apsolut/
.apsolut-agents/
.gstack/

# Env
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Debug dumps (OpenRouter raw responses on failure)
.debug/

# Local runtime logs (fetch output, etc.)
.logs/

# Dashboard captures from `npm run shot` and from browser sessions. Gitignored so a
# screenshot of a real deployment cannot be committed by accident. README and docs images
# are the deliberate exception and live in docs/images/, which is tracked.
.screenshots/
screenshots/

# Analyst briefs. This comment used to claim they were "committed to git … (3) git
# history for long-term archive" while the `briefs/*.md` rule below ignored them and
# zero were ever tracked. Corrected 2026-09-25: there are TWO durability layers, not
# three — the `briefs` table in the signal store (canonical) and the local markdown
# mirror under `briefs/`.
#
# They stay out of git deliberately, for the same reason as `battlecards/*.md`: a brief
# is generated analysis naming real competitors, and a public repo should carry the
# engine and the demo roster, never a deployment's conclusions about named companies.
# A private fork that wants git as a third layer can delete the `briefs/*.md` rule below.
#
# Scheduled cron runs use --force so daily/weekly briefs UPSERT a single file per day
# instead of accumulating time-suffixed variants and blowing up the folder. Ad-hoc runs
# without --force still get time-suffixed filenames (intentional: don't clobber a brief
# you're about to pin).

# The local libSQL database. `store.mjs` defaults to data/signals.db so a fresh clone
# works with no account — that file is a user's own signal store, never repo content.
data/*.db
data/*.db-shm
data/*.db-wal
# Zone 1 Gmail local store + DPAPI token blob (never commit)
data/email/

# Your own roster / feeds. These OVERRIDE the shipped defaults and are deliberately
# untracked, so customising what you track never conflicts with `git pull`.
config/*.local.mjs

# Runtime cache / legacy import staging
data/legacy-jsonl/
data/*.json
data/*.jsonl
data/transcripts/
data/snapshots/
data/trends/
data/talk-tracks/
!data/.gitkeep

# Chrome extension generated data (user-specific, regenerated via `npm run chrome-data`)
chrome-extension/data/companies.json

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Generated artifacts now live in Turso (sql/009-artifacts.sql) — the markdown
# on disk is a local mirror for editing, not the source of truth. Kept OUT of
# git so a public repo carries the engine and demo config, never a deployment's
# competitive analysis, kill shots or customer names. battlecards/_template.md
# is force-added as the shape reference.
battlecards/*.md
!battlecards/_template.md
briefs/*.md


# The static dashboard snapshot from `npm run demo:html` — ~4 MB, regenerated on demand
# and fully derived from the store. Committing it would add a multi-megabyte binary-ish
# blob to history on every refresh, and it carries whatever roster the store held at
# build time. Publish it as a release asset or host it; do not version it.
demo/signal-demo.html

# The current handoff note. Live operations detail — deploy state, credential incidents,
# spend, Gmail volumes — and it points at maintainer notes a clone has no copy of. Kept
# on disk for local use; kept out of git because it is an internal status report, not
# documentation.
HANDOFF.md


# Empty lockfile for an external skills manager; nothing in this repo reads it.
skills-lock.json

# Operator runbook skills — NOT product skills. These encode one deployment's incident
# history (a specific dead credential, specific bad-row counts and dates), its Railway
# setup, and paths into the maintainer's private notes. `/signal-scan` and friends stay
# tracked because they describe how the PRODUCT works; these describe how this
# INSTALLATION is run, which is useless to a clone and discloses more than it teaches.
.claude/skills/signal-publish-prep/
.claude/skills/signal-key-restore/
.claude/skills/signal-repo-hygiene/
.claude/skills/signal-viewer-gate/
.agents/skills/signal-publish-prep/
.agents/skills/signal-key-restore/
.agents/skills/signal-repo-hygiene/
.agents/skills/signal-viewer-gate/
